<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Favorite Athletes! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Famous Panamanians</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> My Favorite Athletes!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write instructions for a simple game or craft. Interactive: Write simple comments on a peer's short text.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write clear instructions for a game about identifying famous Panamanians and their achievements, including opening and closing steps (e.g., "Step 1: Each player draws a card. Step 2: Give clues about the figure's achievements. Step 3: Reveal the correct answer.")<br>Can write brief, supportive comments on a peer's text about a famous Panamanian (e.g., "Roberto Duran is considered one of the greatest boxers in the world." "I enjoyed reading about Roberto Duran.")<br>Can provide feedback on a classmate's description of a notable figure (e.g., "I didn't know Mariano Rivera won so many awards.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage engages students, activates prior knowledge, and introduces the topic through modeling and clarification.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of 3-4 famous Panamanian athletes (e.g., Roberto Durán, Mariano Rivera, Atheyna Bylon). Students work in pairs to write down one sentence describing an achievement for each athlete. For example: "Roberto Durán won many boxing titles." "Atheyna Bylon achieved a medal." This activates prior knowledge and sets a writing-focused tone.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write simple instructions using sequence words (e.g., "First, take a card. Next, describe the person. Finally, guess their name."). The teacher writes on the board, enunciating clearly and highlighting '-ing' verb endings and '-ed' in past tense when describing achievements. For example: "She is <b>training</b> for a new tournament. He <b>performed</b> excellently." The teacher also models phrases for comparing figures: "Roberto Durán is <b>better known</b> than other boxers. He is considered one of the <b>greatest</b> boxers in the world."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'First, draw a card,' what do you do first?" (Elicit: "Draw a card.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'won many awards' tell us about an athlete?" (Elicit: "They achieved a lot / were successful.")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage introduces new language and content in an authentic context related to the scenario "Famous Panamanians."</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents two short texts on the board or projector:
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Text 1:</b> Simple instructions for a game called "Guess the Panamanian Athlete" (e.g., "Step 1: Each player takes a card with an athlete's name. Step 2: Give clues about their achievements. Step 3: Other players guess the athlete. Step 4: The first to guess correctly wins an award point.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Text 2:</b> A brief descriptive paragraph about a famous Panamanian athlete (e.g., "Mariano Rivera is a famous baseball <b>athlete</b>. He <b>achieved</b> many records and <b>won</b> several <b>awards</b>. He is considered one of the <b>best</b> baseball players in history. Many fans enjoyed his <b>performance</b>. He inspired many young players. He is <b>more popular</b> than other players in his position.").</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing achievements of celebrities and developing reports on notable figures. Writing clear, sequential instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the texts silently.
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What kind of texts are these?" (Elicit: "Game instructions and a description of an athlete.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Mariano Rivera is an entertainer." (False) "Step 2 is to give clues." (True)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on accuracy, allowing students to practice the target language through controlled and semi-controlled writing activities.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing instructions, describing achievements, using comparatives and superlatives.
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present continuous for future events, Comparatives and superlatives, Past simple for achievements.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> entertainer, athlete, achievement, award, singer, actor, recognition, competition, performance, sports fan, win, achieve, inspire, train, perform, compete, famous, popular, talented, skilled, specialized, more, best.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice pronunciation of -ing verb endings and -ed in past tense (e.g., "watching," "performed"). Phonemic awareness: Practice reading inflectional endings (-ed, -ing) in sentences (e.g., "He improved his own marks.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Describing achievements ("Mariano Rivera won many awards."), Comparing figures ("Sech is more popular than other singers.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Describing achievements of celebrities and developing reports on notable figures (e.g., "She is Atheyna Bylon. She is a famous boxer and an Olympic medalist.").</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice – Instruction Sequencing):</b> The teacher provides a set of scrambled sentences that form game instructions (e.g., "the correct answer / Reveal", "a card / draws / Each player", "clues about / achievements / Give"). Students work individually to reorder them into logical steps. Teacher checks answers as a class, focusing on initial capitalization and punctuation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Practice – Describing Achievements):</b> Students are given a list of Panamanian athletes and a few keywords for each (e.g., "Irving Saladino - long jump / win gold medal / Olympic Games"). In pairs, they write 2-3 sentences describing each athlete's achievements using past simple, comparatives, and superlatives. Example: "Irving Saladino <b>won</b> a gold medal in the Olympic Games. He is one of the <b>most famous</b> athletes for his long jump <b>achievement</b>." Teacher circulates, offering support and correcting errors.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Guided Sentence Writing – Comparisons):</b> Students complete sentences comparing two athletes or entertainers using "more...than" or "the most..." For example: "Sech is _______ (popular) _______ other singers." "Roberto Durán is considered _______ (great) boxer in Panama." Students write these sentences and then share with a partner to check for accuracy in grammar and vocabulary.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage encourages students to use the language freely in a realistic communicative task, focusing on fluency and task completion.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task:</b> "My Panamanian Athlete Game!" Students will design and write instructions for a simple game about identifying famous Panamanian athletes and their achievements. They will also write comments on a peer's descriptive text about a famous Panamanian.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Written product (game instructions and peer comments).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1: Write Game Instructions (Individual):</b> Students individually write clear, step-by-step instructions for a game about famous Panamanian athletes. The game should involve giving clues about an athlete's achievements (e.g., "Step 1: Each player thinks of a famous Panamanian athlete. Step 2: Write three clues about their achievements using past simple. Step 3: Read your clues aloud. Step 4: Other players guess the athlete. Step 5: The player with the most correct guesses wins."). They should include opening and closing steps and use target vocabulary like 'win,' 'achieve,' 'award,' 'competition,' and 'performance.'</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2: Write a Short Descriptive Text (Individual):</b> Students write a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) describing their favorite Panamanian athlete, including their achievements, using comparatives/superlatives where appropriate. (e.g., "She is Atheyna Bylon. She is a famous boxer and an Olympic medalist. She won a bronze medal in the Olympic Games. She is training for a new tournament. She is a very skilled athlete.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 3: Peer Feedback (Interactive Writing):</b> Students exchange their descriptive texts (from Part 2) with a classmate. Each student reads their peer's text and writes two brief, supportive comments or questions. (e.g., "Roberto Durán is considered one of the greatest boxers in the world. I enjoyed reading about him." or "I didn't know Mariano Rivera won so many awards. Great information!"). The teacher reminds students to use positive and encouraging language.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage involves formative assessment of student learning and provides opportunities for self-reflection.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher uses an observation checklist and collects student written products (game instructions and peer feedback comments) for a quick review. The checklist focuses on the presence of clear steps, target grammar (past simple, comparatives/superlatives), and vocabulary, as well as constructive peer comments.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their written game instructions and the short descriptive text with peer comments.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and logical sequence of game instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (athlete, achievement, award, win, perform, famous, skilled, etc.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of past simple for achievements.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of comparatives and superlatives in descriptions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Brief, supportive, and relevant comments on peer's text.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about writing the game instructions?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words or grammar did you use today?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage allows students and teachers to reflect on the learning process and plan for future lessons.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did writing instructions help you understand the achievements of famous Panamanians better?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you learned today that you can use in your daily life?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this writing lesson? Students were engaged in both instruction writing and peer feedback. The use of visuals and a familiar theme ("My Favorite Athletes!") helped with content generation. What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with consistently applying '-ed' endings in past simple verbs; targeted review or a quick grammar sprint on regular/irregular past verbs might be beneficial. The next lesson (Mediation & Integration) will build on this writing foundation by having students summarize information and integrate their descriptions into a larger project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The instructions and descriptions written today will serve as foundational content for Lesson 5, where students will mediate information about famous Panamanians and consolidate their knowledge into a final project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>